GEVCU7 unit tests
=================

These tests run on the development machine (PlatformIO "native" platform),
not on the Teensy. They compile the real, hardware-independent firmware
sources against small host stand-ins for the Teensy core and peripherals.

Running
-------
    pio test -e native                     # whole suite
    pio test -e native -f test_prefhandler # one suite
    pio test -e native -v                  # show each test's output

Plain `pio test` also works: the teensymm environment ignores all tests.

Layout
------
test_intel_hex/          Intel HEX record parsing used by the firmware updater
test_md5/                MD5 used to verify ESP32 images
test_pid/                PID controller
test_crash_breadcrumbs/  Breadcrumb encode/decode in CrashHandler
test_memcache/           EEPROM write-back page cache (aging, eviction, flushing)
test_prefhandler/        Device table and hashed key/value settings storage
test_faulthandler/       Persistent circular fault log
test_throttle/           Pedal mapping, regen, creep, smoothing, config save/load
test_device/             Device lifecycle and StatusEntry

native/GevcuHostStubs/   Host stand-ins, built as a library for the native env:
  include/Arduino.h        core API, String, Print/Stream, virtual millis()
  include/TeensyThreads.h  Mutex/Scope (records nesting), threads.delay()
  include/FlexCAN_T4.h     CAN frame structs only
  include/SD.h             FsFile reading from an in-memory string
  src/host_arduino.cpp     virtual time + Wire backed by an emulated 256KB
                           24-series I2C EEPROM (page writes, 4 banks)
  src/host_logger.cpp      Logger that records messages for assertions
  src/host_system.cpp      recording fakes for TickHandler, DeviceManager,
                           CanHandler; defines memCache
  include/host_test_support.h  test controls: hostResetAll(), EEPROM
                           peek/poke/fault injection, log and registration
                           inspection
  include/gevcu_test.h     include this from tests

Writing a test
--------------
Create test/test_<name>/test_<name>.cpp, include "gevcu_test.h" and the firmware
header, call hostResetAll() in setUp(), and write a main() that runs the tests
(see any existing suite). If the code under test lives in a source file not yet
compiled for native, add it to build_src_filter in [env:native] in
platformio.ini; if it needs a peripheral or singleton that isn't stubbed, add a
stand-in under native/GevcuHostStubs. The host build uses -funsigned-char to
match ARM, so char behaves as it does on the Teensy.

Not covered here: code that talks directly to i.MX RT1062 registers or real
buses (CanHandler, sys_io, FlasherX/FlashTxx, ESP32 loader, SerialConsole) and
the individual device drivers. Those need on-target or hardware-in-the-loop
testing.
